From: BeatLink <pearson.pryce.p@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: ip5xxx: report the keyboard pack as a device battery

UPower sums every system-scope battery into its composite display device,
so the shell showed a single percentage spanning the phone's 12Wh cell and
the keyboard case's 25Wh pack -- 63% while the phone itself was at 26%. It
takes the scope from the sysfs `scope` attribute, which this driver never
exported, and a missing attribute is treated as system scope.

The pack in the keyboard case powers a peripheral, which is what
POWER_SUPPLY_SCOPE_DEVICE describes. Reporting it leaves UPower listing the
pack as its own device while keeping it out of the total, so the top bar
shows the phone's own charge.

MODEL_NAME comes along because both batteries were otherwise anonymous in
Settings, with nothing to say which was which.

Fork-specific: ip5xxx is a generic power-bank driver, so upstream would want
the scope to come from the device tree rather than be hardcoded.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/ip5xxx_power.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/ip5xxx_power.c
@@ -288,6 +288,8 @@
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE_MAX,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ENERGY_FULL_DESIGN,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ENERGY_EMPTY_DESIGN,
+	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_MODEL_NAME,
+	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_SCOPE,
 };
 
 static int ip5xxx_battery_get_status(struct ip5xxx *ip5xxx, int *val)
@@ -448,6 +450,14 @@
 		return ret;
 
 	switch (psp) {
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_MODEL_NAME:
+		val->strval = "Keyboard";
+		return 0;
+
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_SCOPE:
+		val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_SCOPE_DEVICE;
+		return 0;
+
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_STATUS:
 		return ip5xxx_battery_get_status(ip5xxx, &val->intval);
 
